  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Friday/... \nHemispheric pattern amplification driven by an unusually early \nseason sudden stratospheric warming (SSW) event will be the main \nstory for the latter half of next week with large ensemble forecast \nspread persisting. For much of the week, northern stream jet energy \nwill settle over southern Canada while weak low amplitude waves in \nthe southern stream remain sequestered over northern Mexico. As a \nresult, subsident ridging with nearly steady midtropospheric heights \nwill define the pattern over the SW Conus yielding high confidence \nof a very tranquil, near persistence forecast carrying on from early \nin the week. By the end of the week, effects from disruption of the \npolar vortex will be manifested in poleward retrogressive ridging \nwhile deep negative height anomalies descending equatorward over the \nNE Conus and East Pacific. However, the specifics regarding depth, \npositioning, and evolution of features within the greater synoptic \npattern remain highly uncertain. Recent operational models peel a \nnotable shortwave from the east Pacific trough towards the SW \nConus/northern Mexico late in the week, however this type of \nsolution is not consistent among NAEFS and EPS members. Given the \namplification phase, wide ensemble spread and large run-to-run \nvariations are likely the next several days resulting in low \nforecast confidence for next weekend.",
